\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Task Formulation}
\label{sec:task}

Given a text $x$, the goal is to predict its \emph{generation depth} $d \in \{0, 1, 2{+}\}$, where $d{=}0$ denotes human-written text, $d{=}1$ denotes first-generation LLM output conditioned on human text, and $d{=}2{+}$ denotes text produced by recursively conditioning an LLM on its own prior output at least twice.
Formally, let $\mathcal{C}_0$ be a reference corpus of human-written text.
A generation chain of depth $k$ is defined recursively: $x^{(0)} \sim \mathcal{C}_0$, and $x^{(k)} = G(x^{(k-1)})$ for $k \geq 1$, where $G$ denotes stochastic LLM generation conditioned on its input.
We frame depth estimation as an ordinal classification task over three classes rather than binary real-vs-synthetic detection, enabling finer-grained provenance characterization.
Depths beyond 2 are merged into a single $2{+}$ class, consistent with monotonic feature convergence that compresses inter-class margins beyond depth 2 (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}).

\subsection{Generation Chain Construction}
\label{sec:generation}

We construct generation chains using eight generators spanning five model families: \textbf{Pythia} (2.8B, 1B) \cite{pythia}, \textbf{OLMo} (OLMo-2-1B) \cite{olmo}, \textbf{Qwen} (Qwen3-1.7B, Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen3-8B-Base) \cite{qwen3}, \textbf{Llama} (Llama-3.2-3B) \cite{llama3}, and \textbf{Mistral} (Mistral-7B-v0.3) \cite{mistral7b}.
This selection covers parameter scales from 0.6B to 8B and diverse pretraining corpora, enabling evaluation of cross-generator and cross-scale generalization.

For each generator, we sample approximately 1{,}000 seed passages from English Wikipedia as $\mathcal{C}_0$.
The same pool of seed passages serves as gen-0 for all generators.
We use shared seeds across generators so that cross-generator comparisons control for input content and isolate the effect of the generation model from source text variation.
In LOGO evaluation, gen-0 samples from the held-out generator's chains also appear (with the same text) in the training generators' chains, creating a potential train--test overlap for the gen-0 class.
We address this with a supplementary binary evaluation excluding gen-0 entirely (\S\ref{sec:results-baselines}).
Each seed is iteratively fed through the generator to produce a chain of depth 4 (i.e., gen-0 through gen-4), using the prompt ``\texttt{Continue the following text: \{text\}}''.
All generation uses temperature $\tau{=}0.7$, nucleus sampling with $p{=}0.9$, and maximum length of 256 new tokens.
The theoretical yield is $8 \times {\sim}1{,}000 \times 5 = 39{,}815$ texts; after removing 2{,}746 chain breaks (empty or degenerate outputs at intermediate depths that prevented deeper generation) and 11 passages too short for feature extraction, 37{,}058 texts remain (Appendix~\ref{app:dataset-stats}).
For classification, depths 2--4 are collapsed into the $2{+}$ class.

\subsection{Feature Extraction}
\label{sec:features}

The seven features are motivated by two complementary views of distributional drift.
Reference-model features capture how the text's token-level surprise distribution shifts relative to a fixed model; surface features capture how lexical diversity contracts under recursive generation.
Together, they span both the generative mechanism (what the model produces) and the surface manifestation (how the vocabulary changes).
We use interpretable distributional statistics rather than learned representations so that the DDR tri-partition (our primary contribution) remains transparent; neural features would yield a black-box classifier.
This two-pronged design is validated through leave-one-feature-out analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:lofo}) and cross-group ablation (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}); both analyses confirm that the two feature groups contribute to depth discrimination.

We extract seven distributional features per text, organized into two groups based on what they measure rather than how they are computed.

\paragraph{Reference-model features.}
A held-out reference model (Pythia-410M, chosen to be smaller than all generators) computes per-token negative log-likelihood (NLL) for each text.
We use ``entropy'' as shorthand for token-level NLL throughout, following convention in prior detection work; these are point-wise NLL values, not Shannon entropy of the full distribution.
Although Pythia-410M shares the GPT-NeoX architecture with two of the eight generators, cross-family reference model experiments (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}) confirm that depth features are not reference-specific (LOGO F1 varies by $\leq$0.013 across Pythia, OLMo, and Qwen references).
From the resulting NLL sequence, we extract five statistics:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{entropy\_mean}: mean token-level NLL, which captures the average predictability of the text under the reference model;
    \item \textbf{entropy\_std}: standard deviation of token-level NLL, which measures the spread of per-token surprise;
    \item \textbf{entropy\_max}: maximum token-level NLL, which captures the most surprising token;
    \item \textbf{entropy\_min}: minimum token-level NLL, capturing the reference model's confidence floor on the most predictable token;
    \item \textbf{tail\_decay}: slope of the vocabulary-level averaged log-probability distribution in the mid-rank region (ranks 100--1000), capturing how steeply probability mass decays beyond high-frequency tokens.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Surface features.}
Two features capture lexical diversity without requiring a reference model:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{TTR}: type-token ratio (number of unique word types divided by total tokens);
    \item \textbf{hapax\_ratio}: proportion of words appearing exactly once (\emph{hapax legomena}).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
The seven features were selected a priori from two complementary perspectives (reference-model NLL distribution and surface lexical diversity) to enable interpretable decomposition of depth signal; exploring richer feature spaces (syntactic complexity, semantic coherence) may improve classification but at the cost of the location/shape analysis that is the paper's primary contribution.
These features exhibit a \emph{differential depth response} (DDR) into drifting, intermediate, and near-stationary groups (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}), motivating their combination (pairwise correlations in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-feature-correlation}).
TTR and hapax ratio are strongly correlated ($r = 0.97$); both are retained for marginal LOFO gains.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:evaluation}

\paragraph{Cross-validation.}
Our experiments use three overlapping generator subsets reflecting chronological expansion: the original 5-generator set (Pythia-2.8B/1B, OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-1.7B/0.6B) for cross-model chains, a 6-generator set (adding Qwen3-8B) for appendix analyses, and the full 8-generator set (adding Llama-3.2-3B and Mistral-7B-v0.3) for single-generator LOGO. Each table notes its subset.
To evaluate generalization to unseen generators, we use \textbf{Leave-One-Generator-Out (LOGO)} cross-validation over all eight generators.
LOGO holds out individual generators but does not isolate family effects (e.g., when Pythia-1B is held out, Pythia-2.8B remains in training).
\textbf{Leave-One-Family-Out (LOFO)} addresses this by holding out entire model families, confirming cross-family generalization (average binary F1\,=\,0.686; \S\ref{sec:results-classification}).

\paragraph{Classifier.}
We use XGBoost \cite{xgboost} throughout ($n\_\text{estimators}{=}200$, $\max\_\text{depth}{=}6$, learning rate $0.1$; standard defaults), with identical hyperparameters across all methods in Table~\ref{tab:baselines}.
LOGO F1 is stable across random seeds (std\,$<$\,0.001 over 5 seeds); this near-zero variance reflects the deterministic LOGO splits (each generator held out exactly once) with seeds controlling only XGBoost's internal stochasticity. Bootstrap CIs on held-out folds provide the relevant generalization uncertainty.
Alternative classifiers and ordinal-specific methods (CORAL \cite{coral_ordinal}, CORN \cite{corn_ordinal}, Frank-Hall \cite{frank_hall_ordinal}) yield comparable performance (Appendix~\ref{app:classifier}); discriminative power therefore resides in the features, not the classifier.

\paragraph{Metrics and statistical testing.}
The primary metric is \textbf{macro-averaged F1} over the three depth classes.
We report 95\% confidence intervals via percentile bootstrap ($n{=}1{,}000$), McNemar tests for pairwise comparisons, and paired bootstrap for baseline comparisons.
All $p$-values are two-sided; Bonferroni correction is applied within each family of simultaneous tests: the 21 distributional comparisons (7 features $\times$ 3 depth-group pairs, \S\ref{sec:results-differential}) and the 4 pairwise baseline comparisons (\S\ref{sec:results-baselines}).
Feature--depth correlations (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}) use class-balanced subsamples to control for depth-class imbalance.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare the 7-feature ensemble against four single-score detection methods repurposed as depth predictors: Avg-LogProb, FastDetectGPT \cite{fast_detectgpt}, Binoculars \cite{binoculars}, and LD-Score \cite{ld_score}.
Each baseline score is used as a univariate feature in the same XGBoost framework under LOGO cross-validation to ensure a controlled comparison.

\paragraph{Length confound control.}
Since TTR and hapax\_ratio are mechanically length-dependent, we verify depth signal is not a length artifact via length-matched subsampling that equalizes length distributions across depth classes (\S\ref{sec:results-classification}; Appendix~\ref{app:length}).

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We introduced generation depth classification and identified a differential depth response (DDR) tri-partition across eight base-model generators from five families.
A 7-feature ensemble exploiting this tri-partition achieves leak-free binary F1\,=\,0.694 (LOGO) and 0.764 (cross-model chains).
Current applicability is restricted to base models; instruction tuning and larger scales remain open.

\section*{Ethics Statement}
\label{sec:ethics}

All texts analyzed in this work are LLM-generated from publicly available Wikipedia seeds; no personal or sensitive data is involved.
Our method estimates the generation depth of a text passage (how many recursive generation steps it has undergone), not the identity of its author.
Individual authorship attribution is outside both the scope and the capability of this approach; privacy implications are minimal.
We acknowledge that depth estimation tools could in principle be used to selectively suppress machine-generated text in contexts where such content is legitimate (e.g., accessibility tools, assistive writing).
We encourage deployment in data governance pipelines where provenance transparency, rather than content removal, is the objective.
All code, data, and trained models will be released upon publication.

\paragraph{Licenses.}
All generator models are publicly available under permissive licenses (Apache 2.0 for Pythia, OLMo, and Mistral; Qwen License for Qwen3; Llama Community License for Llama 3.2).
Wikipedia seed passages are distributed under CC BY-SA 3.0.

\paragraph{Compute budget.}
All experiments were conducted on 4$\times$ NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs (32\,GB each), totaling approximately 62 GPU-hours.

\paragraph{Software.}
Experiments used Python 3.12.3, PyTorch 2.10.0 (CUDA 12.8), Transformers 4.57.6, XGBoost 3.2.0, scikit-learn 1.8.0, NumPy 2.2.6, pandas 3.0.2, and SciPy 1.17.1.

